## 2. Categories to Sweep (systematically, not spot-check)

### 2a. Seed/Demo Data Leaking Into Production Code Paths
- [ ] Search for any array/object literal containing what looks like real product names, customer names, employee names, or transaction amounts inside `/apps/web` or `/apps/api` source files (NOT inside a clearly-marked `/seed` or `/fixtures` directory used only for local dev/testing).
- [ ] Confirm seed scripts (Prompt 02's role-seeding script, any demo-data generators) are isolated to a dedicated seed/migration path and NEVER imported into a production request-handling code path.
- [ ] Search for hardcoded tenant names, subdomains, or tenant IDs anywhere outside of test files — a hardcoded tenant ID in application logic is a severe violation (it would literally only work correctly for one specific tenant).

### 2b. Business Configuration Hardcoded Instead of Database/Settings-Driven
- [ ] Tax rates, currency symbols, or currency formatting hardcoded instead of pulled from `tenant.default_currency`/`tenant.default_timezone` (Prompt 02, Prompt 05 Section 6) — grep for hardcoded `"Rs."`, `"%"`, `"PKR"`, hardcoded decimal tax multipliers.
- [ ] Reorder thresholds, discount limits, approval spend-thresholds (Prompt 13's PO approval logic) hardcoded as a number in logic rather than read from a tenant settings table.
- [ ] Warehouse/branch names, mill names hardcoded in any conditional logic — every reference to a specific location must be a database lookup by ID, never a string comparison against a hardcoded name.
- [ ] Vendor names, customer names, or supplier-specific logic hardcoded (e.g., `if (vendor.name === "ABC Textiles") { applySpecialDiscount() }`) — business rules like this must be data-driven (a discount rule record), never a name-string branch in code.

### 2c. Roles & Permissions Hardcoded Instead of Database-Driven
- [ ] Cross-check every role/permission check in the codebase against Prompt 03's RBAC model — confirm permission checks query the `role_permissions` table (or its cached representation) at runtime, and are NOT a hardcoded `if (role === "owner" || role === "gm")` string-comparison chain scattered through business logic. A hardcoded role-name chain is fragile (breaks the moment a tenant creates a custom role per Prompt 10 page 83) and defeats the entire purpose of the configurable Roles & Permissions Manager.
- [ ] Confirm the 11 system roles from Prompt 02/03 exist as actual DATABASE ROWS (seeded), not as a hardcoded array referenced directly by application logic in place of a database query.
- [ ] Frontend: confirm role-based UI visibility (Prompt 12, Section 7) checks the user's actual fetched permission set from the API, not a hardcoded frontend role-to-feature mapping that could drift out of sync with the backend's real enforcement.

### 2d. Product/Inventory/Pricing Data
- [ ] Zero product names, SKUs, prices, or variant data (size/color options) hardcoded anywhere in application code — every single one must originate from the `products`/`product_variants`/`stock_levels` tables (Prompt 02).
- [ ] Dropdown option lists in forms (Prompt 08-11's pages) — confirm category dropdowns, unit-of-measure dropdowns, etc. are populated from database-backed lookup tables or tenant-configurable settings, not a hardcoded frontend array, UNLESS the list is a genuine fixed system enum (Section 1's "not a violation" example — e.g., the 5 production stages, which are a true fixed workflow, not tenant-configurable business data).

### 2e. User-Facing Text Masquerading as Data
- [ ] Distinguish between legitimate i18n string constants (fine — UI labels like "Submit," "Cancel" belong in the i18n system per Prompt 05 Section 6, not the database) versus BUSINESS content hardcoded as if it were UI text — e.g., a hardcoded list of "leave types" or "defect categories" in a translation file is actually tenant-configurable business data mistakenly treated as static UI copy, and should be a database-backed lookup table instead.

### 2f. API Keys, Credentials & Environment-Specific Values
- [ ] Zero API keys, database connection strings, JWT signing secrets, or third-party credentials (payment gateway, SMS provider, FBR tax API — per Prompt 03 Section 5, Prompt 10 page 86) hardcoded anywhere in source code — confirm every one is loaded exclusively from the secrets manager/environment variables (this overlaps with Prompt 03's security audit but deserves its own explicit sweep here since it's the highest-severity hardcoding violation possible).
- [ ] Confirm no `.env` file with real secrets is committed to the repository (check git history too, not just the current working tree — a secret once committed remains in history even after later removal, and must be rotated, not just deleted).

### 2g. Mock/Placeholder Data Left in Production Paths
- [ ] Search for common mock-data leftover patterns: `TODO: replace with real data`, `// temporary`, `// hardcoded for now`, `FIXME`, `mockData`, `dummyData`, `placeholderProducts` — these comments/names are exactly the trail a rushed implementation leaves behind, and are a fast, high-signal way to find real violations.
- [ ] Confirm no frontend component has a fallback hardcoded array that silently renders when an API call fails or returns empty — this masks real bugs (a broken API call looks like it's "working" because fake data fills the gap) and must instead show the proper empty/error state from Prompt 12, never synthetic data standing in for real data.

