## 5. Legitimate Exceptions — Do NOT Flag These As Violations

To avoid a noisy report full of false positives, explicitly exclude:
- True system-level enums that represent the platform's own fixed workflow logic (production stage names, POS payment-method type identifiers, HTTP status codes) — these are code, not tenant business data, and are correctly hardcoded as enums (though even here, if a FUTURE requirement makes them tenant-configurable — e.g., a tenant wanting a custom 6th production stage — that's a scope decision to flag for product discussion, not an audit violation today).
- UI microcopy/labels genuinely covered by the i18n system (Prompt 05, Section 6) — this is the correct home for those strings, not the database.
- Local development seed/fixture data that is clearly isolated to a `/seed`, `/fixtures`, or `/dev` directory and demonstrably never imported into any production request-handling path — confirm this isolation explicitly rather than assuming it from folder naming alone.
- Genuine infrastructure/environment configuration (port numbers, timeout values, retry counts) that are legitimately code-level operational constants, not business data — though even these are better served from environment variables/config than magic numbers scattered in logic, per general code-quality practice, just not the SAME severity as business-data hardcoding.

