## 6. Severity Classification for Findings

| Severity | Criteria | Example |
|---|---|---|
| **CRITICAL** | Hardcoded value would cause real cross-tenant incorrect behavior or a security issue | Hardcoded tenant ID, hardcoded API credential, hardcoded role-bypass logic |
| **HIGH** | Hardcoded value would produce visibly wrong business behavior for any tenant other than the one it was written against | Hardcoded tax rate, hardcoded branch name in conditional logic, hardcoded pricing |
| **MEDIUM** | Hardcoded value works today but will silently break under a specific future/edge scenario | A dropdown list that should be tenant-configurable but currently has only one tenant's values, hardcoded discount threshold |
| **LOW** | Technically a violation of the "should be data-driven" principle but low real-world impact | A hardcoded fallback string that only shows in a rare edge case, not user-facing business data |

---

## 7. Report Requirements

`HARDCODED_DATA_AUDIT.md` must include:
1. Executive summary: total findings by severity.
2. Full findings table: file path, line number, code snippet, severity, required fix.
3. Results of the three behavioral tests in Section 3 (change-it-and-see, new-tenant, different-country) — pass/fail with specifics, not just a summary judgment.
4. A prioritized remediation order: CRITICAL and HIGH findings block release readiness; MEDIUM/LOW are tracked as follow-up tickets.
5. An explicit confirmation statement (or explicit list of exceptions) that the 11 system roles, all product/pricing data, all tax/currency logic, and all branch/warehouse references were verified to originate from the database — this is the specific claim the user needs confirmed, so it must be answered directly and explicitly, not left implied by the absence of findings.

**Do not mark this audit "passed" based on absence of obvious violations alone — actually perform the behavioral tests in Section 3.** A codebase can look clean in a text search while still having subtle hardcoded assumptions that only the "change it and see" and "new tenant" tests will actually reveal.

